<GuidePage title='Containers & Content Shifting - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 10 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
  <h1>Containers &amp; Content Shifting</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Containers are the known half of "contain the unknown": they render immediately, they reserve
    accurate space, and everything uncertain happens <em>inside</em> them. Get this right and nothing
    on the page ever moves; get it wrong and every request landing is a small earthquake.
  </p>

  <h2 id='cost'>10.1 What content shifting costs</h2>
  <p>
    Content shift is the page rearranging itself while the user is already using it. The costs are
    concrete: the paragraph they were reading jumps out from under their eyes; the button they were
    about to press moves and they press what replaced it; scroll position stops meaning anything
    while the page is still "settling." Each shift also re-runs layout for everything below it. On
    long pages, repeatedly. And users read instability as unreliability: a page that jolts three
    times before it settles <em>feels</em> broken even when every request succeeded.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is measured in the field as <strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)</strong>, one of Google's
    Core Web Vitals: every unexpected shift is scored by how much of the viewport it moved, and the
    scores accumulate. But the metric is just the symptom sheet. The disease has one cause:
    <strong>something rendered without its space having been reserved.</strong> And SPA-style pages have
    it worst, because when all data arrives after hydration (<a href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a>), <em
      >every region</em
    > is a late arrival landing in unreserved space, one request at a time, in whatever order the network
    decides.
  </p>

