    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The flex-grow-and-scroll frame from the snippet above, on the page. The app layout owns the
      chrome: the app header bar across the top and the sidebar down the left.
      <code>ReportsLayout</code> is the dashed region beside the sidebar and beneath the app header: a
      column that clips itself (<code>h-full max-h-full overflow-hidden</code>). Inside it, <code
        >&lt;ReportsHeader /&gt;</code
      > is a page-level header (not the app header), sized by its content; the main region takes <code
        >grow</code
      > and
      <code>overflow-y-auto</code>, stretching to fill everything beneath the reports header and
      scrolling its rows internally when they overflow. The chrome, the frame, and both headers stay
      put no matter how much data lands. Color coding: gray is app chrome (context, not this
      chapter's subject), blue is the known frame that
      <code>ReportsLayout</code> owns and reserves, and amber is the unknown: the rows that stream into
      the scroll region later, landing inside space that never moves.
