  <h3 id='route-groups'>Route groups: peers share one layout</h3>
  <p>
    The same persistence extends to <strong>siblings</strong>, and this is where it's most often
    thrown away. Routes like <code>/learners</code>, <code>/evaluators</code>, and
    <code>/levels</code> are not nested under each other; they're peers. A <strong
      >route group</strong
    > (a parenthesized folder that adds no URL segment) exists precisely so peers can share one layout:
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> define the header, the tab bar, and the table container once in the group's <code
      >layout.tsx</code
    >, and all three routes render inside it. Navigating between them swaps each page's table and
    moves the active-tab highlight (a one-leaf <code>useSelectedLayoutSegment</code> read,
    <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#tabs-layout'>§9.3</a>), while the frame never repaints.
    Being siblings rather than children is not a reason to skip the shared layout; it's the reason
    to reach for the group. The common layout should be leveraged as much as humanly possible, never
    repeated redundantly at the top of each sibling page, where every navigation would tear it down
    and rebuild it.
  </p>

