  <h3 id='layout-hierarchy'>The layout hierarchy: shared chrome, declared once</h3>
  <p>
    Route groups are one instance of a rule that governs the whole app directory:
    <strong
      >chrome is composed by nesting layouts, and each piece of it is declared exactly once, in the
      layout at the level where it applies.</strong
    >
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The root layout (<code>app/layout.tsx</code>) owns the app-wide shell: the
    header bar, the primary sidebar, whatever frames every screen. A section's layout owns that
    section's chrome (a secondary nav, a section header, a tab bar). A page owns only its content.
    Each layout renders its own chrome plus <code>{'{'}children{'}'}</code>, and the next layer down
    (a nested layout, then a page) renders into that slot. The header and sidebar are written once,
    in the root, and every page in the application renders within them.
  </p>

