  <p>
    The failure mode is re-declaring the shell lower down: a page (or, worse, every nested layout)
    imports <code>&lt;AppHeader /&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Sidebar /&gt;</code> and wraps its own content
    in them. It looks like it works, and it is three kinds of wrong. It <strong
      >re-mounts the shell on every navigation</strong
    > instead of letting it persist (<a href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'>§3.1</a>), so
    the header and sidebar visibly rebuild as the user moves around. The copies <strong
      >drift</strong
    >: a change to the header lands in the routes that remembered to update and not the others. And
    where a page sits under a section layout that <em>also</em> renders the shell, the app paints the
    header <strong>twice</strong>.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/reports/page.tsx - the shell, wrongly re-declared in a page</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* AppHeader and Sidebar are app-wide chrome. Rendering them here
     re-mounts them on every navigation and forks a private copy that
     drifts from every other page's. A page should return content. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"app-shell"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">AppHeader</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span> /&gt;
      &lt;main <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"min-h-0 grow overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportsTable</span> /&gt;
      &lt;/main&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Every page that does this owns a private copy of the app shell. The header and sidebar rebuild
      on each navigation instead of persisting, and the copies drift the moment one route updates
      the header and the rest do not.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The fix is to hoist each piece of chrome to the highest layout that needs it and let composition
    do the rest. The root owns the shell; a section layout owns its own header and tabs; the page is
    a leaf.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/layout.tsx - the app shell, declared once for every route</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">RootLayout</span>({ children }: <span class="tok-fn">LayoutProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;html <span class="tok-prop">lang</span>=<span class="tok-str">"en"</span>&gt;
      &lt;body&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">AppHeader</span> /&gt;
        &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-0 grow"</span>&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Sidebar</span> /&gt;
          <span class="tok-com">{/* Every route renders here. A navigation swaps this slot;
              the header and sidebar persist, never re-rendering. */}</span>
          &lt;main <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"min-h-0 grow overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;{children}&lt;/main&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/body&gt;
    &lt;/html&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The app shell exists in exactly one file. Every page in the app is the <code
        >{'{'}children{'}'}</code
      >
      here, so it renders inside the header and sidebar without knowing they exist, and a navigation leaves
      them untouched (<a href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'>§3.1</a>).
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/layout.tsx - the section's chrome, declared once</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramLayout</span>({ children }: <span class="tok-fn">LayoutProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-0 grow flex-col"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramTabs</span> /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* reports / people / settings fill this slot; each returns
          only its own content, never the header, tabs, or app shell */}</span>
      {children}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The section adds only its own chrome and nests inside the root's slot;
      <code>reports/page.tsx</code> is then a two-liner that returns
      <code>&lt;ReportsTable /&gt;</code> and nothing else. The rule, stated plainly: if you are importing
      the app header or sidebar anywhere other than the one layout that owns it, that is the bug. It belongs
      in an ancestor layout, and the page belongs in that layout's
      <code>{'{'}children{'}'}</code>. Same statically-knowable composition as
      <a href='03-mental-model.html#app-directory'>§3.2</a>: the folder tree is the layout tree.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    This is also the bridge to <a href='14-blocking-requests.html'>§14</a>: once containers live in
    the layout, each one can be filled independently: its own data, its own Suspense boundary, its
    own error state. That's exactly what parallel routes formalize (<a
      href='14-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'>§14.3</a
    >): slots in the layout's scaffold, filled as independent units.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Containers are
          <strong>known, server-rendered, and painted with the first response</strong>: chrome and
          dimensions both.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          The loading state <strong>never includes the container</strong>; the suspended component <strong
            >never renders its own container</strong
          >.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Fallback and content <strong>share one footprint</strong>: the reservation is honest, and
          the swap moves nothing.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          List and table pages use the <strong>flex-grow-and-scroll frame</strong>: the page never
          changes height; growth happens inside the scroll region.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Media always declares its dimensions</strong> (<code>next/image</code>,
          <code>aspect-ratio</code>); fonts load via <code>next/font</code>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Containers render <strong>as high as their stability allows</strong>: usually the layout,
          so navigation reuses them.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

